🏷️ Vulnerability Title

Insecure Storage of Encrypted Credentials with Co-located Key Material

⚠️ Risk Level

High

📊 CVSS 3.1 Score (Intranet Context)

CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:L → 8.2 (High)

📂 Vulnerability Category
Insecure Cryptographic Storage
Sensitive Data Exposure
Security Misconfiguration
🧩 OWASP Category

OWASP Top 10 2021 – A02: Cryptographic Failures

📖 Description

It was observed that the application stores encrypted credential files (.enc) along with associated key files (.txt) and configuration files (DBAccessConfig.xml) within an accessible network share directory.

The presence of encryption keys or key-related material in the same location as the encrypted data significantly weakens the effectiveness of the encryption mechanism. An attacker with access to this directory may be able to leverage the available key material and application logic to decrypt sensitive credentials.

💥 Impact
Unauthorized access to database credentials
Potential decryption of stored passwords
Exposure of backend systems and financial data
Increased risk of lateral movement within the internal network
Compromise of confidentiality and integrity of sensitive data
🛠️ Recommendations

It is recommended to implement secure credential and key management practices by ensuring that encryption keys are stored separately from encrypted data.

It is recommended to implement access control mechanisms to restrict access to sensitive directories and files based on the principle of least privilege.

It is recommended to implement secure secrets management solutions such as centralized key vaults or secrets managers instead of storing credentials in file systems.

It is recommended to implement encryption using industry-standard practices where key management is handled securely and independently.

It is recommended to implement regular audits to identify and remove sensitive information from shared locations.

